hadiths[1]{hadithnumber,text}:
"3050","**\nNarrated to us Bundār, narrated to us Muḥammad ibn al-Minhāl, narrated to us Yazīd ibn Zurayʿ, narrated to us Shuʿba, from al-Aʿmash, from Abū Ḍabyān, from Ibn ʿAbbās (RA) that the Prophet (ﷺ) said:\n\n*\If a child performs Hajj"","" it counts as Hajj for him until he reaches the age of discernment (puberty). When he reaches the age of discernment"","" he must perform another Hajj. If a Bedouin performs Hajj"","" it counts as Hajj for him. But when he migrates (to Madīna)"","" he must perform another Hajj.\""*\n\nNarrated to me Bundār and Abū Mūsā (RA)"","" they both said: Narrated to us Ibn Abī ʿAdī"","" from Shuʿba"","" from Sulaymān"","" from Abū Ḍabyān"","" from Ibn ʿAbbās (RA) in a similar narration"","" but *mawqūf* (stopped at the Companion).\n\nAbū Bakr (ibn Abī Shayba) said: *\""This is my knowledge"","" and it is correct beyond doubt.\""*\n\nHe further said: *\""This wording: 'If a Bedouin performs Hajj...' is from the type of narration that I used to say applies at certain times rather than all times. If this wording is authentic from the Prophet (ﷺ)"","" then this ruling was before the conquest of Makka by the Prophet (ﷺ). When he conquered Makka and declared that there is no migration after the conquest"","" the Bedouin and the migrant became equal in the obligation of Hajj. Thus"","" if a Bedouin performs Hajj"","" it suffices for him just as it suffices for the migrant"","" due to the abrogation of migration after the conquest of Makka.\""*"""""
